nife: the capability core runs on RISC-V.

soak: started 4 groups of one responder, 3 callers, 1 grinder and 1 tick waiter (24 user threads) on 4 online core(s), beating every 5s
soak: silence longer than a few beats is a hang, not a slow run; the beat is on the wall clock and does not depend on the workload making progress
soak: a clean run is a number to compare against, NOT evidence that the concurrency is correct (design/roadmap/219-a-workload-that-does-not-stop.md)
soak: the threads that cross cores are the tick waiters, NOT the rendezvous pairs; a rising crossings count is the wake protocol sustained across cores under load, not the IPC workload migrating (design/roadmap/221-a-soak-that-crosses-cores.md)
soak: rounds counts IPC round trips and wakes counts tick-route wakes; they are separate because they are separate quantities, and neither rate compares with a soak built differently
soak-census: where the kernel placed each worker at spawn: R=responder, C=caller, G=grinder, W=tick waiter, and the number after each letter is its group
soak-census: core=0 threads=5 R0 C1 C1 C2 C3
soak-census: core=1 threads=6 C0 C0 C1 G1 C2 C3
soak-census: core=2 threads=6 C0 W0 R1 R2 C2 R3
soak-census: core=3 threads=7 G0 W1 G2 W2 C3 G3 W3
soak-census: this is the boot-time lottery and NOT where the run settles: a rendezvous wake queues the peer on the waker's own core (DECISIONS 138, how a saturated workload is made to hand threads across cores), so each group converges onto one core within a few exchanges
soak-census: the beat line's drifted= is how many responders, callers and grinders are no longer on the core the census above put them on; while it reads 0 that census is current, and a nonzero one is followed by the census that replaces it
soak-census: tick waiters are excluded from drifted= because their movement is the point rather than a surprise, and it is already crossings= (design/roadmap/221-a-soak-that-crosses-cores.md)
